    {
      "id": "claim_37c601234dcc4412ae7180712a4a44ff",
      "statement": "Sandboxing is defense in depth, not a security boundary against a determined local attacker: the Java code runs in a JVM-in-WASM inside a Worker inside an iframe with no host filesystem or OS processes (and file/process APIs blocked by the import ban before compilation), network limited to what the browser fetch stack permits from that origin, and nothing to protect the visitor from except runaway/misbehaving submissions handled by the timeout and teardown; there is no cross-visitor or server-side data at risk because there is no server.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/ARCHITECTURE.md#L182-L203",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:f2e9c5754d2a78a9c3da56ecc49682b721b248e0f139044b3bca0080636a9bad:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_098912c9168e4510b1be323a5d3a41b7",
      "statement": "Code-task execution depends on two distinct third-party artifacts under different licenses and distribution shapes: the CheerpJ runtime (proprietary, CheerpJ Community License) is loaded live from the CDN via importScripts('https://cjrtnc.leaningtech.com/4.3/loader.js') in sandbox-worker.js and must never be vendored/self-hosted (the Community License forbids it), while public/tools.jar (~18 MB, OpenJDK 8 tools.jar, GPLv2 + Classpath Exception) is shipped as a static asset and put on the compile classpath (/app/tools.jar:/files/) for in-browser javac; both are one-time browser-cached downloads, and the CheerpJ loader is pinned to a version but not hash-verified because importScripts in a Worker supports no integrity attribute. Code tasks require network access to cjrtnc.leaningtech.com; text/choice katas have no such dependency.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/ARCHITECTURE.md#L122-L132",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a810265430471774e1db26602688e75d8f321b48664ba3886c95aef832604244:"
